1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Camouflage suits and invisible planes.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:07,000
Loading art and scanning technology.

3
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:10,000
Hidden cities and serious makeup.

4
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:14,000
Now, Camouflage on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,000
These US Marines are conducting an ambush training exercise.

6
00:00:37,001 --> 00:00:44,000
They're masking their presence by practicing ancient camouflage techniques.

7
00:00:44,001 --> 00:00:51,000
Painted faces, silent signals, outlines broken by cut branches.

8
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:01,120
Search team off!

9
00:01:04,000 --> 00:01:08,120
Ancient camouflage techniques helped this ambush exercise to succeed.

10
00:01:10,080 --> 00:01:15,140
But there's something strikingly modern about this ambush that contributed greatly to its success.

11
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:20,860
These Marines are wearing a new computer-generated digital camouflage uniform.

12
00:01:23,060 --> 00:01:28,360
This distinctive design of colors and shapes breaks up a warrior's outline in a unique way.

13
00:01:28,361 --> 00:01:34,800
The digital pattern of tiny green, black and khaki squares is a collection of specs up close.

14
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:39,220
But from a distance, it combines with the surrounding environment.

15
00:01:40,360 --> 00:01:43,800
The Marine Corps leadership fast-tracked the creation of the uniform.

16
00:01:44,280 --> 00:01:44,980
Move out!

17
00:01:46,700 --> 00:01:51,820
The project progressed from idea to initial distribution in just 18 months.

18
00:01:52,340 --> 00:01:55,400
The fastest uniform development in U.S. military history.

19
00:01:58,360 --> 00:02:03,240
Most Marines felt a change in uniform was not only necessary, but perhaps long overdue.

20
00:02:04,160 --> 00:02:09,860
The one the Marine Corps used for the past 20 years was made up of geometric shapes and continuous lines.

21
00:02:10,380 --> 00:02:15,180
Those shapes and lines attract the human eye naturally, which makes it easier to detect.

22
00:02:15,181 --> 00:02:21,040
It was designed during the Cold War in which the threat was a Soviet threat rolling into Europe.

23
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:22,460
That threat has gone away.

24
00:02:22,461 --> 00:02:28,760
A Marine now can be deployed anywhere from the mountains of Afghanistan to the tropics in South America.

25
00:02:29,180 --> 00:02:34,200
We had to come up with a new pattern that would work in a multi-array of environments.

26
00:02:35,120 --> 00:02:41,680
The Army Soldier System Center in Natick, Massachusetts, carried out the laboratory or scientific work.

27
00:02:44,160 --> 00:02:50,440
Over a hundred conceptual uniform patterns were initially entered into Natick's highly developed computerized design system.

28
00:02:52,460 --> 00:02:57,120
The intuitive three-dimensional software program tested thousands of variables,

29
00:02:57,720 --> 00:03:02,440
and only 25% of the time required just a few years ago for creating test designs.

30
00:03:04,560 --> 00:03:08,960
Once you have it in there, you can reduce the colors in the scene, you can add colors,

31
00:03:09,620 --> 00:03:13,480
you can cut and paste in all kinds of editing features.

32
00:03:14,500 --> 00:03:19,940
Designs that passed the first cut were sent to one of the world's largest six-color digital inkjet printers.

33
00:03:19,941 --> 00:03:30,040
A revolutionary system of 64 ink nozzles per color prints a vividly detailed camouflage pattern one line at a time.

34
00:03:32,660 --> 00:03:37,900
Swatches for each test pattern and for each potential environment were made into prototype uniforms.

35
00:03:38,840 --> 00:03:42,860
The final uniform designs were ultimately narrowed to several samples.

36
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:47,560
This is the desert pattern for, obviously, desert scenario.

37
00:03:47,561 --> 00:03:52,120
This here is the woodland pattern for jungles, woodland areas.

38
00:03:52,740 --> 00:03:55,800
And these two are developmental for the urban patterns.

39
00:03:58,140 --> 00:04:05,840
The uniform samples were then examined by the naked eye for the two basic elements of camouflage clothing, color and pattern.

40
00:04:07,100 --> 00:04:10,900
Colors were tested to see if they matched the predominant colors of the surrounding environment.

41
00:04:12,520 --> 00:04:16,780
The shapes of the patterns were checked to see if they helped hide the contours of the body.

42
00:04:17,560 --> 00:04:27,920
If you have a color in the uniform that's on the edge of the uniform that's similar to the background, it'll break up that area of the outline.

43
00:04:29,220 --> 00:04:32,980
The infrared properties of the dyes in the uniform were also checked.

44
00:04:34,580 --> 00:04:42,940
It's a general principle of camouflage to provide effective near-infrared camouflage protection against being detected through night vision devices.

45
00:04:42,941 --> 00:04:50,500
Anti-infrared dyes in the pattern make it hard for night vision devices to focus on the uniform.

46
00:04:52,080 --> 00:04:56,940
At ranges of less than 100 feet, only 80% of the uniform is visible.

47
00:04:57,900 --> 00:05:01,440
It is completely invisible at ranges of 200 feet or more.

48
00:05:03,500 --> 00:05:05,380
And it's generally in terms of lightness, darkness.

49
00:05:05,980 --> 00:05:09,240
If something appears very, very bright in a relatively dark background,

50
00:05:09,241 --> 00:05:12,480
well, you haven't got the right infrared properties in that particular color.

51
00:05:12,940 --> 00:05:18,380
The next stop after Natick finished its evaluation was the Marine Scout Sniper School,

52
00:05:18,381 --> 00:05:23,120
where field trials were conducted by the Corps' premier experts in camouflage.

53
00:05:23,121 --> 00:05:28,960
When they took it out to the field to test it, some of the snipers felt that this ground

54
00:05:28,961 --> 00:05:34,400
shade, which they are calling coyote, needed to go redder, which would be closer to dirt,

55
00:05:34,401 --> 00:05:36,460
bark, that kind of thing.

56
00:05:36,461 --> 00:05:38,980
This was an original pattern we started with.

57
00:05:38,981 --> 00:05:42,700
You can see how the colors have gone from light green to more of a coyote.

58
00:05:44,080 --> 00:05:48,440
The new uniform's camouflage properties passed field testing with flying colors.

59
00:05:49,540 --> 00:05:54,780
The uniform was immediately put into production using high-speed, advanced methods of textile manufacturing.

60
00:05:56,200 --> 00:05:59,040
Production of a uniform is quite complex.

61
00:05:59,041 --> 00:06:02,720
It's not just a matter of taking scissors and cutting it.

62
00:06:02,860 --> 00:06:04,380
There's a lot of automation involved.

63
00:06:04,381 --> 00:06:08,820
American Apparel in Selma, Alabama

64
00:06:08,821 --> 00:06:11,320
offered both speed and quality control

65
00:06:11,321 --> 00:06:14,180
with their laser-driven high-volume sewing machines

66
00:06:14,181 --> 00:06:19,380
Uniforms were assembled five times faster

67
00:06:19,381 --> 00:06:22,760
and with far fewer mistakes than traditional hand methods

68
00:06:22,761 --> 00:06:27,480
There's actually something very unique about this uniform

69
00:06:27,481 --> 00:06:30,020
that has never been done in any other uniform

70
00:06:30,021 --> 00:06:36,140
because of the pride and the heritage of the Marine Corps and the esprit.

71
00:06:36,700 --> 00:06:40,880
We have built in every 18 inches a small eagle globe and anchor.

72
00:06:43,840 --> 00:06:46,680
Modern technology produced the Marines' new uniform,

73
00:06:47,360 --> 00:06:53,300
but the camouflage principles behind it were learned instinctively from nature thousands of years ago.

74
00:06:53,301 --> 00:07:00,300
Much of our camouflage that we use today is based on our observations of nature.

75
00:07:00,301 --> 00:07:05,300
Very clear, nature gives us so many powerful signs.

76
00:07:05,301 --> 00:07:10,300
Ancient hunters were the first to read nature's signs.

77
00:07:10,301 --> 00:07:18,300
They discovered that the colors of many animals graduated from dark on their backs to lighter shades on their undersides.

78
00:07:18,301 --> 00:07:22,680
This graduation tended to break up an animal's outline

79
00:07:22,681 --> 00:07:25,480
and even out its three-dimensional surface,

80
00:07:25,940 --> 00:07:28,020
making it harder to see as one object.

81
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,020
You look at a leopard and you see those spots,

82
00:07:30,120 --> 00:07:31,440
you go, how can he hide from anybody?

83
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:34,540
But when he's moving through the grasses of the savannah

84
00:07:34,541 --> 00:07:38,140
or out in the open, those spots blur

85
00:07:38,141 --> 00:07:41,080
and create a type of camouflage

86
00:07:41,081 --> 00:07:44,300
that hides the leopard from his intended victim.

87
00:07:48,300 --> 00:07:52,220
Early hunters quickly learned from the animal kingdom how to create their own deception.

88
00:07:53,800 --> 00:07:59,280
African tribesmen deceived their prey and their enemy by wearing bird feathers to break up their silhouettes.

89
00:08:00,840 --> 00:08:04,020
Native Americans disguised themselves by dressing in buffalo skins.

90
00:08:06,080 --> 00:08:10,940
But Native Americans also noticed that bison herds never worried about wolves.

91
00:08:12,100 --> 00:08:16,700
And consequently they started wearing wolves clothing and went hunting buffaloes.

92
00:08:16,701 --> 00:08:18,900
they were allowed to get very close enough to kill them.

93
00:08:21,400 --> 00:08:25,920
In ancient warfare, camouflage was limited to putting on cut branches

94
00:08:25,921 --> 00:08:28,600
and wearing colored cloth to match the environment.

95
00:08:31,340 --> 00:08:34,920
Galleons were painted blue by Roman sailors to match the ocean.

96
00:08:36,740 --> 00:08:39,680
But most ancient commanders did not use camouflage.

97
00:08:41,460 --> 00:08:45,500
Ancient and medieval warfare really didn't concern themselves with camouflage,

98
00:08:45,501 --> 00:08:51,860
and there was a major reason for that, and it was the style of warfare during the time period.

99
00:08:53,200 --> 00:08:56,360
The tools of war at the time had a very limited reach.

100
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:59,300
As their armies rushed head-on against each other,

101
00:09:00,120 --> 00:09:03,540
commanders were more concerned with sorting out who belonged to which side.

102
00:09:05,000 --> 00:09:08,280
To determine who was who in these close-range combat fields,

103
00:09:08,940 --> 00:09:11,740
leaders dressed their men in showy, recognizable uniforms.

104
00:09:11,741 --> 00:09:20,800
It was only in the 18th century, when longer-ranged rifles were introduced, that military leaders

105
00:09:20,801 --> 00:09:23,860
slightly reduced the garishness of the way they dressed their soldiers.

106
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:30,560
But this departure from gaudy to subdued attire was reserved mostly for scouts and snipers.

107
00:09:35,220 --> 00:09:40,980
Among the most famous scouts were a band of American rangers, led by Major Robert Rogers.

108
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:47,400
During the French and Indian War of 1754, they were the Green Berets of colonial America.

109
00:09:48,460 --> 00:09:54,380
The colonial woodsmen were quite adept at camouflage and stalking in the forest because of their vast hunting experience.

110
00:09:55,220 --> 00:09:58,900
During the war, they used the same experience against the French and Indians,

111
00:09:59,400 --> 00:10:05,020
by wearing browns and greens, sometimes even camouflaging their faces with mud or dirt,

112
00:10:05,360 --> 00:10:06,880
delaying wait for the enemy.

113
00:10:06,881 --> 00:10:14,500
But for the rest of the 18th century and throughout the 19th century, ordinary soldiers were led

114
00:10:14,501 --> 00:10:19,620
into battle wearing bright uniforms and marching behind multicolored flags.

115
00:10:23,320 --> 00:10:27,152
While close combat dominated ground fighting during the U.S.

116
00:10:27,153 --> 00:10:28,940
Civil War, Confederate ships

117
00:10:28,941 --> 00:10:34,000
loaded with war materials used the colors and conditions provided by nature to conceal

118
00:10:34,001 --> 00:10:36,972
their movement from Union observers.

119
00:10:36,973 --> 00:10:41,100
These blockade runners would time their arrival at

120
00:10:41,101 --> 00:10:47,641
the proper tide and the proper moon so that all the environment was darkened.

121
00:10:47,642 --> 00:10:49,340
They could slip into

122
00:10:49,341 --> 00:10:54,212
a port without being caught by the federal blockaders.

123
00:10:54,213 --> 00:10:57,280
Battlefield dress changed slightly

124
00:10:57,281 --> 00:11:02,020
during the Boer War between the British and Dutch settlers in South Africa in 1899.

125
00:11:02,021 --> 00:11:07,020
Well, the Boer War was kind of akin to the Revolutionary War.

126
00:11:07,021 --> 00:11:09,020
And the British, of course, had the redcoats.

127
00:11:09,021 --> 00:11:11,020
Africanists fought guerrilla-type warfare,

128
00:11:11,021 --> 00:11:15,020
and they just picked off the British awful easy.

129
00:11:15,021 --> 00:11:21,020
It was right after that war that they decided to go to an olive drab uniform.

130
00:11:21,021 --> 00:11:25,020
The American army also changed to a khaki uniform in 1898

131
00:11:25,021 --> 00:11:30,020
while fighting its colonial war with the Spanish and the Philippines.

132
00:11:30,021 --> 00:11:33,980
but the rest of the world's military stuck with vivid shades of grays and greens.

133
00:11:35,980 --> 00:11:38,840
It would take the outbreak of a savage world war

134
00:11:38,841 --> 00:11:41,440
and the introduction of deadly new technologies

135
00:11:41,441 --> 00:11:45,520
before military leadership began to accept the idea of camouflage.

136
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:51,380
Next, zebra-striped warships, reversible camouflage suits,

137
00:11:51,760 --> 00:11:53,600
and buildings disguised as trees.

138
00:11:55,160 --> 00:11:58,900
The term camouflage comes from the French word camoufler,

139
00:11:58,901 --> 00:12:01,060
meaning to blind or veil.

140
00:12:01,660 --> 00:12:04,460
We'll be back with more camouflage on Modern Marvels.

141
00:12:07,900 --> 00:12:10,360
We now return to Modern Marvels.

142
00:12:14,020 --> 00:12:17,000
At the outbreak of World War I in 1914,

143
00:12:17,860 --> 00:12:19,880
most military leaders on both sides

144
00:12:19,881 --> 00:12:21,920
still held to the traditional view

145
00:12:21,921 --> 00:12:24,520
that camouflage on a large scale was unnecessary.

146
00:12:24,521 --> 00:12:33,140
But individual soldiers determined to survive the horror of war took matters into their own hands.

147
00:12:33,141 --> 00:12:39,640
No one prescribed a style of camouflage pattern for the soldiers.

148
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:44,320
They evolved it based on their own experience in the trenches.

149
00:12:46,040 --> 00:12:51,760
Individual soldiers covered their positions with cut foliage or chicken wire stuffed with rags.

150
00:12:52,440 --> 00:12:56,460
They smeared mud on their clothes, faces, and weapons when going on patrol.

151
00:12:57,460 --> 00:13:00,420
To reduce their outline as they peeped above the trenches,

152
00:13:01,300 --> 00:13:05,320
a few on both sides wore helmets hand-painted in geometric patterns.

153
00:13:07,360 --> 00:13:10,460
Some camouflage ideas were more creative than effective.

154
00:13:11,460 --> 00:13:15,940
Like this reptilian-painted suit that was supposed to hide snipers in trees,

155
00:13:15,941 --> 00:13:18,700
but instead gave the snipers position away.

156
00:13:18,701 --> 00:13:25,380
or the overgarments used to hide troops in specific environments, like this woodpile.

157
00:13:26,180 --> 00:13:29,100
Problem was, soldiers could never stay motionless long enough.

158
00:13:31,900 --> 00:13:36,260
By 1914, new technologies introduced very early in the war

159
00:13:36,261 --> 00:13:39,780
changed military leadership's reluctance about camouflage.

160
00:13:42,100 --> 00:13:46,440
Principal among the new technologies were the airplane and aerial photography.

161
00:13:46,441 --> 00:13:55,620
An aircraft could range deep into the adversary's back areas, observe and report and identify future targets.

162
00:13:57,040 --> 00:14:02,640
Camouflage that hit soldiers from an enemy on the ground was ineffective against this new aerial threat.

163
00:14:03,840 --> 00:14:08,920
Troops spotted in rear areas by aerial observers were fired upon by another new technology.

164
00:14:09,980 --> 00:14:13,480
Guns capable of accurately hitting targets 10 miles away.

165
00:14:13,481 --> 00:14:18,680
Rear echelon commanders suddenly developed a keen interest in camouflage.

166
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:24,860
Allied military leaders didn't turn to a hardened warrior for advice,

167
00:14:25,280 --> 00:14:28,480
but rather to American painter and naturalist Abbott Thayer,

168
00:14:29,020 --> 00:14:31,920
best known for his images of idealized winged women.

169
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:35,560
He was a well-known artist at the time,

170
00:14:36,120 --> 00:14:41,060
and he was also known for his interest in nature and natural things

171
00:14:41,061 --> 00:14:44,000
in protective coloration or concealment in nature.

172
00:14:45,260 --> 00:14:48,380
Combining his skill as an artist with his study of wildlife,

173
00:14:49,220 --> 00:14:53,220
Thayer in the early 1890s drew the same conclusions as ancient hunters.

174
00:14:54,320 --> 00:14:57,720
The light and dark colors and shapes of animals and birds

175
00:14:57,721 --> 00:15:00,480
allowed them to blend naturally into the background.

176
00:15:04,740 --> 00:15:08,420
Thayer's theorizing became central in creating military camouflage.

177
00:15:08,421 --> 00:15:12,440
The first to adopt it on a large scale were the French.

178
00:15:14,440 --> 00:15:18,980
Under the supervision of a fashionable Parisian portraitist named Guiron de Savola,

179
00:15:19,980 --> 00:15:25,420
a mobile corps traveled along the front line disguising artillery positions, trains, and airfields.

180
00:15:27,780 --> 00:15:34,640
Among the 10,000 camouflage workers were a large number of artists, architects, painters, and theater set designers.

181
00:15:34,641 --> 00:15:43,360
In 1915, British pilots were the first to experiment with Thayer's camouflage theory of light and dark shading.

182
00:15:44,460 --> 00:15:47,460
When you're a fighter pilot, you've got two goals.

183
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:51,980
Shoot other people in the air and shoot the people on the ground.

184
00:15:52,900 --> 00:15:58,740
Well, you've got to disguise yourself from both people because both people are trying to stop you from doing that.

185
00:15:58,741 --> 00:16:04,640
Pilots painted the bottom of their planes light blue or white to blend in with the sky,

186
00:16:05,300 --> 00:16:08,000
making it difficult for the enemy to see them from the ground.

187
00:16:08,560 --> 00:16:12,100
And they painted the top of their planes brown, to look like the ground,

188
00:16:12,440 --> 00:16:15,560
so that enemy aircraft would have a harder time seeing them from above.

189
00:16:19,760 --> 00:16:21,700
Camouflaging warships proved more difficult,

190
00:16:22,160 --> 00:16:27,040
because they float on a vast empty ocean of gray, blue, or green, depending on the sun.

191
00:16:28,740 --> 00:16:30,920
But there was an urgent need to do something.

192
00:16:33,400 --> 00:16:36,080
U-boats were sinking ships left and right,

193
00:16:36,860 --> 00:16:40,660
and the British needed some way to offer protection to their ships.

194
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:46,000
Aware that totally hiding a ship in an ocean environment was impossible,

195
00:16:46,900 --> 00:16:50,200
Navy planners sought ways to make ships less vulnerable to attack.

196
00:16:50,900 --> 00:16:52,640
They started by looking at the threat.

197
00:16:52,641 --> 00:16:55,740
The submarine was a relatively new weapon,

198
00:16:55,741 --> 00:16:59,020
and so were the torpedoes they carried.

199
00:16:59,160 --> 00:17:01,460
They had to get in line with the target

200
00:17:01,461 --> 00:17:04,640
and literally aim the submarine at the target

201
00:17:04,641 --> 00:17:06,480
before they fired a torpedo.

202
00:17:08,520 --> 00:17:10,740
If they couldn't hide a ship, they thought,

203
00:17:10,860 --> 00:17:13,320
perhaps they might find a way to buy a little time

204
00:17:13,321 --> 00:17:15,940
for a targeted ship to get out of torpedo range.

205
00:17:17,080 --> 00:17:19,940
They picked a variance of Thayer's disruptive pattern

206
00:17:19,941 --> 00:17:21,640
and called it Dazzle.

207
00:17:21,641 --> 00:17:30,300
The purpose of Dazzle is usually to confuse the heading of the ship and confuse the type of the ship.

208
00:17:30,920 --> 00:17:35,860
These gaudy patterns make it very difficult to tell what type of ship it is.

209
00:17:37,220 --> 00:17:41,360
Each ship was painted differently in long vertical and horizontal lines

210
00:17:41,361 --> 00:17:46,100
and varying colors to distort the shape of the ship or the movement of the ship

211
00:17:46,101 --> 00:17:48,880
or where the important areas of the ship would be.

212
00:17:48,881 --> 00:17:55,160
As many as six or seven vivid colors were used to form many geometric shapes all jumbled together.

213
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:01,720
Submarine crews were somewhat confused at first, but quickly readjusted their tactics.

214
00:18:02,920 --> 00:18:07,120
It wasn't all that effective, but the people at the time thought they were well protected.

215
00:18:08,660 --> 00:18:16,360
By the end of World War I in 1918, camouflage for ships, planes and guns had found a permanent place in modern warfare.

216
00:18:18,880 --> 00:18:21,400
And as the world prepared for another world war,

217
00:18:22,140 --> 00:18:25,020
camouflage uniforms were also considered for the first time.

218
00:18:27,820 --> 00:18:31,140
The Nazis, fueled by their vision of world conquest,

219
00:18:31,760 --> 00:18:36,740
plunged headlong into a comprehensive camouflage development program in the mid-1930s.

220
00:18:37,780 --> 00:18:41,420
They did reversible camouflage. They did waterproof camouflage.

221
00:18:42,160 --> 00:18:44,920
They had an octet camouflage they used on the Russian front,

222
00:18:45,020 --> 00:18:47,820
which was white on one side and colored on the other.

223
00:18:48,880 --> 00:18:55,880
In 1937, the American Army took its first serious look at the potential of camouflage uniforms.

224
00:18:55,881 --> 00:19:01,880
In this vintage training film, various camouflage designs are shown being tested.

225
00:19:01,881 --> 00:19:06,880
And while several seemed to hold promise, none were put into production.

226
00:19:06,881 --> 00:19:12,880
American military planners focused their attention on better systems for concealing equipment.

227
00:19:12,881 --> 00:19:18,400
By the time the U.S. entered the Second World War in 1941, they were ready.

228
00:19:19,760 --> 00:19:24,500
